<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 10: Work and Energy in 3D</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 5.3.2 through 5.4. 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Equation 5.15 comes from integrating the net force <m>\vec{F}_{\text{net}}</m> over the displacement of 
            the <alert>center of mass</alert>, <m>d\vec{R}</m>. Is this the same thing as the total work done on 
            each of the particles in the system, added up? Can you think of situations where they might be different?
            What about situations where they are the same? Your examples should involve more than 1 particle.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		In example 5.4 of the conical pendulum, even though the pendulum is swinging, its energy remains 
              constant. What is the role of the forces here? What about the pendulum are they changing?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	In example 5.12, what does the book mean when it says that the boundary conditions are 
            <q>built in</q> to the energy solution? 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Look at problem 5.4 in the homework. What is a choice of system for which the mechanical energy 
                K+U is constant? Make sure to explain why it is constant.    
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Is there a (potentially different) system that has a constant horizontal momentum <m>P_x</m>? 
                For this same system, what can you say about the vertical momentum <m>P_y</m>?  
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Reflection on the above: If you try to use the same system from part (a) to answer part (b), 
                do any difficulties arise? Lesson: we are allowed to use different systems to discuss the same problem, but
                we must be extremely clear about which system we are applying our physical principles to.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          	
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
